'''atheistical''' (ā-thē̤-is′ti-kal), ''a.'' Marked by or manifesting atheism; atheistic.
{{smaller block|I was present, very seldom going to the publiq theaters for many reasons, now as they were abused to an ''atheistical'' liberty.
{{right|''Evelyn'', Diary, Oct. 18, 1666.}}}}

'''atheistically''' (ā-thē̤-is′ti-kal-i), ''adv.'' In an atheistic manner; impiously.
{{smaller block|I entreat such as are ''atheistically'' inclined to consider these things.
{{right|''Tillotson.''}}}}

'''atheisticalness''' (ā-thē̤-is′ti-kal-nes), ''n.'' The quality of being atheistic; irreligiousness.
{{smaller block|Purge out of all hearts profaneness and ''atheisticalness''.
{{right|''Hammond'', Works, I. 500.}}}}

'''atheize''' (ā′thē̤-īz), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''atheized'', ppr. ''atheizing''. [< Gr. ἄθεος (see ''atheism'') + ''-ize''.] '''I.'''† ''intrans.'' To discourse as an atheist.
{{smaller block|We shall now make diligent search and inquiry, to see if we can find any other philosophers who ''atheized'' before Democritus and Leucippus.
{{right|''Cudworth'', Intellectual System, p. 111.}}}}

'''II.''' ''trans.'' To render atheistic. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|They endeavoured to ''atheize'' one another.
{{right|''Bp. Berkeley'', Minute Philosopher, ii.}}}}

'''atheizer''' (ā′thē̤-ī-zėr), ''n.'' One who atheizes, or renders atheistic. ''Cudworth.'' [Rare.]

